Six lines, a first and a spare for each, all under 90 characters, no number, no real person. The first of each is my choice; the editor may swap in the spare.

THE CONDUCTOR (pacing)
  first: One step per wake, on the clock. Small and steady beats one heroic night.
  spare: I don't write the chapters. I make sure someone wakes up to write them.

SECURITY (checking)
  first: Checked: keys, scope, secrets. Not checked: what nobody wrote down. Tell me.
  spare: I read the plan before you start and the diff after. Yes, both. Every time.

THE HOLD (stopping)
  first: Anyone can pull me. Only the editor puts me back.
  spare: I'm not the failure. I'm the brake that let everyone else go fast.

Why these: the conductor's line is the hourly machine's argument (many small steps); security's first admits what a check cannot see, which is the book's point about expectations; the hold's first is its rule, and its spare echoes the talk's close ("brakes so we can go faster") without quoting it.

